contentText:
The BMI Country Awards honored the songwriters and publishers behind many of the year’s biggest country hits on Tuesday (Nov. 17). Held at BMI’s Nashville offices, the annual celebration was what BMI’s CEO Mike O’Neill called &#8220;the biggest parking lot party in Nashville.&#8221;

“You all give us the words we can’t find on our own,” he said to the songwriters in the room.

To honor the massive, genre-shifting success of &#8220;Choosin&#8217; Texas&#8221; — written by Ella Langley, Luke Dick, Miranda Lambert and Joybeth Taylor, and recorded by Langley — the BMI Country Awards presented the writers with the inaugural BMI Impact Award, which recognizes a song, songwriter or artist whose cultural impact has distinguished their creative work as a defining force. &#8220;Choosin&#8217; Texas&#8221; has spent 28 weeks atop the all-genre Billboard Hot 100 so far, holding the all-time record for the most weeks at No. 1 on that chart. To honor the writers, Thomas Rhett took the stage to perform &#8220;Choosin&#8217; Texas.&#8221;

“Much love, y’all, congrats,” he told the writers.

Morgan Wallen and Charlie Handsome were both named BMI country songwriter of the year for cowriting seven of BMI&#8217;s most performed country songs of the year, among them &#8220;I Ain&#8217;t Comin&#8217; Back,&#8221; &#8220;I Got Better,&#8221; &#8220;Superman&#8221; and &#8220;I&#8217;m the Problem.&#8221;

Handsome took the stage and praised Wallen for his win. &#8220;He’s one of the best songwriters I’ve ever met and he deserves recognition … he’s a great songwriter and artist,&#8221; he said.

&#8220;I&#8217;m the Problem&#8221; was named the 2026 BMI country song of the year and is published by Big Loud Mountain, Bo Jewell Club, Ernest Money Music, Honey Hole Publishing, Sony/ATV Songs LLC and Warner-Tamerlane Publishing Corp. The song was written by Grady Block, Handsome, Jamie McLaughlin, Ernest Keith Smith and Wallen. &#8220;I&#8217;m the Problem&#8221; topped the Country Airplay chart for eight weeks, making it one of only six songs in the chart’s history since 1990 to stay at the pinnacle for eight or more weeks.

Warner-Tamerlane Publishing Corp. took home publisher of the year. In the past year, the company published 31 of the most performed songs, among them &#8220;20 Cigarettes,&#8221; &#8220;I&#8217;m the Problem,&#8221; &#8220;Single Again&#8221; and &#8220;After All the Bars Are Closed.&#8221;

Thirty-eight first-time award winners were recognized during the evening, among them Summer Overstreet for Tucker Wetmore’s “3,2,1&#8243;; BigXthaPlug, Krishon &#8220;Bandplay&#8221; Gaines, Jenna Johnson and KK Johnson for “All the Way&#8221;; Jackson Foote, Jordan Gray and Kevin Powers for Jelly Roll’s “Amen&#8221;; Trent Dabbs, Max McNown and Ava Suppelsa for Max McNown’s “Better Me for You (Brown Eyes)&#8221;; Hunter Huff, Alex Maxwell and Ciaran Wilkie for Chase Matthew’s “Darlin’;” and Ty Myers for “Ends of Earth.”

Jeffrey Steele was honored with the Icon Award. Steele has been affiliated with BMI since 1981 and is a two-time BMI country songwriter of the year winner. He&#8217;s earned more than 34 BMI country and pop awards for his deep catalog of songs. During the evening, Bailey Zimmerman performed &#8220;What Hurts The Most&#8221; (a hit for Rascal Flatts) while Florida Georgia Line performed &#8220;My Town&#8221; (recorded by Montgomery Gentry), and Keith Urban and Eric Church reunited to perform their 2013 hit &#8220;Raise &#8216;Em Up.&#8221;

BMI&#8217;s Mike O&#8217;Neill and BMI Icon honoree Jeffrey Steele. Photo courtesy Getty Images for BMI.

“He doesn’t just write songs, he wrestles with life along the way &#8230; the beauty and complexity of what it is to be a human,&#8221; BMI’s Nashville vp of creative, Clay Bradley, said of Steele.

Additionally, tribute videos were shown from Craig Wiseman, Dann Huff, Lee Thomas Miller, Tim McGraw, and Locash.

Taking the stage, Steele praised late music champion Billy Block and remembered a phrase that Billy often said: “If you see somebody without a smile, give them one of yours.”

&#8220;He would always shine that light on people,&#8221; Steele said. He went on to thank many in Nashville&#8217;s songwriting community who have become like family to him over the years. He added: “It’s not about being a star, it’s about being a light &#8230; that’s what songwriters do, being their light to the world.&#8221;
